# First position on the guitar's three thinnest strings: eight notes, G up to G, read from tab, named,
# played, and found again from their letters. The staff beside the tab waits for a decision on how the
# product writes guitar notes (a guitar book writes them an octave above where they sound).
lesson music-notes-from-tab v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=3 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Notes from tab on three strings"
  goal "Read tab on strings 1 to 3, name each note, and play a short line."

  look {
    scene 30x24 {
      column all space=1 align=start at=canvas(1, 1) {
        tab map tuning=guitar notes=["s3f0", "s3f2", "s2f0", "s2f1", "s2f3", "s1f0", "s1f1", "s1f3"] values=[] even=true letters=true ring=-1
        hand h side=left numbers=guitar ring=0 colours=true
      }
    }
    say """
      Mo and Ana are writing tunes for the bandstand on the three thinnest strings. The tab shows
      eight notes going up. String 3 played open is G, and at fret 2 it is A. String 2 open is B,
      fret 1 is C and fret 3 is D. String 1 open is E, fret 1 is F and fret 3 is G. The fret number
      is also the finger that presses it.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.tab-first-position-note count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked music.tab-place-for-note k=1 level=medium
    }
    practice music.tab-place-for-note count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=10
    practice music.tab-higher-note count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=8 hard-seed=4
    practice music.tab-play-line count=1 seed=1
    level hard {
      show music.tab-same-note-two-ways v=1 r=0
    }
    practice music.tab-spells-word count=1 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.tab-same-note-two-ways v=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.tab-fewest-string-changes v=2 easy-v=0 hard-v=4
  }
  remember {
    say "String 1 gives E, F and G, string 2 gives B, C and D, and string 3 gives G and A."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that the three thinnest strings in first position hold eight notes in order, G up to
    G, and that a note can be read from tab or found from its letter. The wrong ideas to watch for
    are reading the bottom line of the tab as string 1, thinking a bigger fret number is always a
    higher note when the two notes are on different strings, and thinking a different string must be
    a different note. In the working, look for the child saying the string and the fret before the
    letter. The two-star question has one note in two places; the three-star question chooses
    between the two places for G so that the word changes string as few times as it can. A child who
    is stuck can say the open strings aloud, E, B and G, and count up the frets from them, and go
    back to the way in; stay on this lesson another day if the letters are still guesses. Off the
    page, play the eight notes up and down on a real guitar, and write a word in tab for someone
    else to read.

    Guitar music on a staff is written an octave above where it sounds, and the staff drawing has no
    guitar clef yet, so this lesson reads tab only.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes to name are on string 1, the place for F is found once in the teacher's pen before E and B, the two notes to compare are on one string, Mo's line goes up the notes in order, the secret word uses strings 1 and 2 only, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star word is BAG."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes to name are on strings 2 and 3, the places to find are C and A, the two notes to compare are on different strings, where the bigger fret number can be the lower note, Mo's line comes down across two strings, E in two places is judged before the two-star's B, the secret word uses string 3, and the three-star word is BADGE."
  }
}
